<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dagobert I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 603–639</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merovingian-dynasty">Merovingian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dagobert I was a Merovingian king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, made king of Austrasia in 623 and ruler of all the Frankish kingdoms from 629 until his death in 639. With advisers such as Eligius he strengthened royal power, and he was a patron of the abbey of Saint-Denis, where he was the first king to be buried. After him real power passed increasingly to the mayors of the palace.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dagobert-i.jpg" alt="Dagobert I" width="320" height="152" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tr%C3%A9missis_de_Dagobert_Ier.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dagobert was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-ii">Chlothar II</a> and Haldetrude and the grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fredegund">Fredegund</a>. Chlothar had reigned alone over all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> since 613 and made his son king of Austrasia, almost certainly to bind the Austrasian nobility to the ruling Franks.</p><p>As a child Dagobert lived under the care of Arnulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> and Pepin of Landen, forebears of the Carolingian dynasty. Chlothar at first excluded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a> from the grant, but the Austrasian nobility forced him to concede them. Dagobert then created a new duchy in the south-west of Austrasia, the later Duchy of Alsace, to guard against Burgundian or Alemannic ambitions, and made his courtier Gundoin its first duke.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On his father's death in 629 Dagobert inherited Neustria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. His half-brother Charibert claimed Neustria, but Brodulf, who petitioned on Charibert's behalf, was assassinated by Dagobert, who became sole king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and later gave Aquitaine to Charibert as a consolation. In 629 he concluded a treaty with the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> that entailed the compulsory baptism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> throughout his kingdom. He protected the markets at Duurstede and Utrecht, raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and helped Sisenand to the Visigothic throne, receiving a golden dish of some 500 pounds. When Charibert and his son Chilperic were assassinated in 632, Burgundy and Aquitaine were firmly under his rule.</p><p>In 631 he led an army against Samo, ruler of the Slavic Wends, after Wends had robbed and killed Frankish merchants. His Austrasian forces were defeated at Wogastisburg, though his Alemannic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> allies repelled the Wends. In 632 the Austrasian nobles rose under the mayor of the palace, Pepin of Landen, and in 634 Dagobert appeased them by making his three-year-old son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigebert-iii">Sigebert III</a> king there. He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> his capital and built the Saint-Denis Basilica.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Dagobert died in 639, aged thirty-six, and was the first Frankish king buried at Saint-Denis, which set a precedent for later French rulers. Most historians consider him the last of the great Merovingian kings. After his death Pepin of Landen recovered his position at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>.</p><p>The historian Patrick Geary called the combined rule of Dagobert and Chlothar the apogee of Merovingian royal power, and the pattern of division and assassination continued until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a> deposed the last Merovingian in 751. A biography, the Gesta Dagoberti, written in the 830s, is mostly unreliable. The nursery rhyme Le bon roi Dagobert, probably of the 18th century, has no historical accuracy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dagobert I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The church grew immensely rich through royal and aristocratic gifts, and monasteries multiplied, especially after the Irish monk Columbanus founded Luxeuil around 590; the abbey of Saint-Denis near Paris, where <b>Dagobert I</b> was buried in 639, became closely tied to the crown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Dagobert I</b>, king of Austrasia since 623, became ruler of Neustria and Burgundy as well, and so of all the Frankish kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-18">18 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The chronicle of Fredegar records that King Judicael met <b>Dagobert I</b> at Clichy in 636 and made peace with the Franks.</blockquote><a href="/state/domnonee">Domnonée</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 633 <b>King Dagobert I</b> appointed Radulf duke to defend the eastern frontier against the Slavs of Samo's Empire, then raiding Thuringia.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-thuringia">Duchy of Thuringia</a></li><li><blockquote>In 631 a dispute over murdered Frankish merchants led <b>King Dagobert I</b> to invade; Samo defeated the Franks at Wogastisburg, and the Sorb prince Dervan then joined him.</blockquote><a href="/state/samos-empire">Samo's Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Saint-Denis, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.94,2.35&amp;year=626" aria-label="Saint-Denis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 455 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Dagobert I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 725h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 725h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=48.94,2.35&amp;year=626">What was here in 626 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
